You should have a look at Roderick Schertler's Hearse page and the 
bones-info Perl script on http://www.argon.org/~roderick/hearse/.  There 
are examples there of what a 'good' bones file should look like.

If you're not a Perlite, you can get NHIDSave from 
http://www.nethack.de/programs.shtml.  I don't think it can interpret 
all the version info, but it can tell you the fingerprint of your bones 
file and you can work back from there.

